+       <tr><td>ScaleLinear · ScaleTemporal</td><td>Shared continuous and time scales (domain, range, zero baseline, timezone).</td><td>viz_scale_* domain / range / clamp / zero_baseline</td><td>VizScaleSummary, VizScaleControls</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>LayoutLayer · LayoutFacet · LayoutConcat</td><td>Layered marks; small-multiple grids; side-by-side panels that share an axis or colour scale.</td><td>viz_layout_type, shared_channels, rows / columns fields …</td><td>—</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>InteractionTooltip · InteractionHighlight</td><td>Hover/tap details; hover or selection emphasis.</td><td>viz_interaction_* fields, trigger</td><td>—</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>Geocodable</td><td>Marks data as mappable; auto-detects latitude/longitude or region identifiers.</td><td>geo_resolution, geo_latitude_field, geo_identifier_field …</td><td>GeocodablePreview, GeoFieldMappingForm, GeoResolutionBadge</td></tr>
+       <tr class="famrow"><td colspan="4">SaaS-billing pack (4, beta; Refundable experimental)</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>SaaSBillingBillable</td><td>Plan price configuration: cadence, currency, trial policy.</td><td>plan_code, amount_minor, billing_interval, trial_period_days, collection_method</td><td>—</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>SaaSBillingMetered</td><td>Usage metering with included/consumed quantity and overage.</td><td>meter_name, included_quantity, consumed_quantity, overage_rate_minor, samples</td><td>—</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>SaaSBillingPayable</td><td>Invoice terms, status, balance, overdue age buckets and payment-retry attempts.</td><td>invoice_number, status, due_at, total_minor, balance_minor, aging_bucket_days</td><td>—</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>SaaSBillingRefundable</td><td>Refund eligibility windows and credit-memo tracking.</td><td>refundable_until, total_refunded_minor, credit_memo_balance_minor</td><td>—</td></tr>
+     </tbody>
+   </table></div>
+   <p class="note">The four SaaS-billing traits live in a <em>domain pack</em> (<span class="k">domains/saas-billing/</span>) — a folder of traits and objects for one vertical that the engine loads alongside the core set. That is the mechanism for adding a new domain without touching core traits.</p>
+
+   <!-- 4 -->
+   <h2 id="objects"><span class="n">04</span>Objects and composition</h2>
+   <div class="def"><b>Object</b><p>A YAML file naming a thing you model, listing the traits it has (each with its parameter values), and declaring the handful of fields that are genuinely its own. Objects do not hold records — they hold the definition Forge builds screens, charts and types from.</p></div>
+   <p>The Product object (<span class="k">objects/core/Product.object.yaml</span>), in its real shape with most traits elided:</p>
+ <pre><code>object: { name: Product, domain: core.catalog }
+ traits:
+   - name: content/Labelled
+     alias: ProductLabel
+   - name: lifecycle/Stateful
+     parameters: { states: [draft, in_review, active, retired], initialState: draft }
+   - name: financial/Priceable
+     parameters: { supportedCurrencies: [USD, EUR, GBP, AUD], defaultCurrency: USD,
+                   pricingModels: [one_time, subscription, usage_based] }
+   <span class="cmt"># + Timestampable, Classifiable (taxonomy), Searchable, Filterable, Pageable</span>
+ schema:   <span class="cmt"># Product's own fields</span>
+   product_id, sku, inventory_status, release_channel, requires_subscription, support_level, summary_blurb</code></pre>
+   <p>An object may also carry its own <span class="k">semantics</span> and <span class="k">tokens</span> sections, which override the traits'.</p>
+   <h3>What composition does</h3>
+   <p>When Product is composed:</p>
+   <ol>
+     <li><b>Fields are merged</b> — every trait's schema fields plus the object's own: 38 fields for Product. If two traits declare the same field, the full engine applies collision rules (same type → merge, keep the stricter; two enums → union; required beats optional; different types → error unless the object resolves it) and records which trait each field came from.</li>
+     <li><b>Parameters are applied</b> — validated against each trait's schema, and parameter-driven enums become real constraints (Product's generated TypeScript type has <span class="k">status: 'draft' | 'in_review' | 'active' | 'retired'</span>).</li>
+     <li><b>UI contributions are collected per context</b> — all traits' view extensions grouped by context and sorted by priority, giving a ready list of what to show on each kind of screen.</li>
+     <li><b>Tokens, semantics, actions and (at most one) state machine are merged.</b></li>
+   </ol>
+   <p class="note">Two composers exist. The full engine (<span class="k">src/core</span>, <span class="k">src/registry</span>; the <span class="k">object:*</span> commands) does all four steps. The tools' runtime composer (<span class="k">packages/mcp-server</span>; what <span class="k">object show</span> and <span class="k">design.compose</span> use) merges schema, semantics, view extensions and tokens only — last-wins on collisions with a warning, no parameter validation — and orders extensions higher-priority-first (default 0), where the React view engine orders lower-first (default 50).</p>
+   <p>For Product, the collected contributions are concretely:</p>
+   <div class="tw"><table>
+     <thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Components Product gets, from its traits</th></tr></thead>
+     <tbody>
+       <tr><td>list</td><td>LabelCell, SearchInput, FilterPanel, PaginationBar, StatusBadge, RelativeTimestamp, PriceBadge, ClassificationBadge</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>detail</td><td>DetailHeader, StatusTimeline, PriceSummary, ClassificationPanel, AuditTimeline, SearchInput, FilterPanel</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>form</td><td>FormLabelGroup, StatusSelector, ClassificationEditor</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>card</td><td>CardHeader, StatusBadge, PriceCardMeta</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>timeline</td><td>TimelineEntryLabel, StateTransitionEvent, AuditEvent</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>inline</td><td>InlineLabel</td></tr>
+     </tbody>
+   </table></div>
+   <p>That composed record is what the screen composer, the chart composer, the code generator and <span class="k">pnpm object:generate</span> (TypeScript interfaces, one per object, into <span class="k">generated/objects/</span>) all read. Change Product's trait list and every one of them changes with it. From the terminal: <span class="k">pnpm object:compose Product</span> prints the merged fields with their source trait; <span class="k">pnpm object:create Widget --trait lifecycle/Stateful</span> scaffolds a new object.</p>
+
