+   <p class="note">The saas-billing pack also defines its own Subscription (Stripe/Chargebee-shaped, with metering); because <span class="k">objects/</span> is scanned before <span class="k">domains/</span>, the tools expose the core Subscription under that name. Adding an object is a new <span class="k">*.object.yaml</span> file (the suffix is required) in <span class="k">objects/</span> or a domain pack; the loaders index once per process, so it appears on the next server start.</p>
+
+   <!-- 6 -->
+   <h2 id="contexts"><span class="n">06</span>Contexts, regions and components</h2>
+   <div class="def"><b>Context</b><p>The kind of screen an object is being shown in. A trait's view extension names the context it targets, so the same object produces a different — but consistent — screen for each. Traits contribute to six: <b>list</b>, <b>detail</b>, <b>form</b>, <b>timeline</b>, <b>card</b>, <b>inline</b>; two more, <b>chart</b> and <b>dashboard</b>, exist as view shells.</p></div>
+   <p>Every context in Forge's component library is laid out on the same six named regions, in this order: <span class="k">globalNavigation</span>, <span class="k">pageHeader</span>, <span class="k">breadcrumbs</span>, <span class="k">viewToolbar</span>, <span class="k">main</span>, <span class="k">contextPanel</span>. Main is always required; page header is required in every context except card and inline; list and timeline also require the toolbar. Regions render as real HTML landmarks (nav, header, main, aside with labels), so structure is visible to screen readers and testable.</p>
+   <p><b>How a screen is assembled.</b> For the requested context, Forge collects every trait's extensions, sorts them by priority (lower first, default 50; ties by declaration order), evaluates any conditions (data, permissions, theme), groups them by region and composes them in a fixed order. Each rendered piece is wrapped in a boundary carrying its id, region and source trait, and a debug mode prints a per-region report. Below tablet width (768px container) the two-column contexts collapse to one column; an opt-in drawer component turns the side panel into a focus-trapped sheet.</p>
+   <p class="note">There are two renderers. The React <b>view engine</b> (the component library, Storybook, the region contract above) and the tools' <b>HTML renderer</b>, which places the same trait contributions into layout templates — dashboard, form, detail, list, card, timeline, landing — with named slots (header, tab-N, metadata, items, filters, hero, cta …). Generated HTML documents carry the tokens and components, not yet the six-region responsive shell.</p>
+   <h3>Components</h3>
+   <p>The canonical intake contains 109 unique component claims. The historical reconciliation proposal classified 98 as runtime component rows (15 native and 83 recipes) and 11 as non-runtime authoring-only rows. Derek accepted retaining all 109 obligations in decision #1788; the old 98-runtime split remains unapproved and approvedRuntimeCensus stays null. Missing implementations remain obligations. Sprint-182 evidence marks exactly 14 React and 14 Vue surface cells as <span class="k">foundation-v1-candidate</span>; a separate independent review accepted those candidates and a distinct authorization promoted all 28 to <span class="k">foundation-v1</span>. That promotion does not approve the 98-row runtime census. That historical foundation nucleus is Badge, Banner, Button, Card, Checkbox, DatePicker, Grid, Input, Select, Stack, Table, Tabs, Text and Textarea. Sprint 193 now measures 109 React and 109 Vue implementations, 109 HTML mappings, and 109 versioned contracts. Accessibility and theme are verified for 109 rows; interaction is verified for 40 and explicitly not applicable for 69 static rows. No framework row is unavailable. These are measured component scenarios and bounded runtime receipts; Sprint 193 remains built work pending independent review. The generated capability ledger and date-tagged catalog export agree. The current proposal is 24 native, 84 recipe and 1 alias; all 109 classifications await Derek’s approval; the independent Sprint 192 review certified the sprint (decision #1891) without approving the proposal. <span class="k">catalog.list</span> filters the catalog by category, trait or context; <span class="k">object show</span> returns an object's composed traits and extensions.</p>
+
+   <!-- 7 -->
+   <h2 id="tokens"><span class="n">07</span>Tokens, themes and brands</h2>
+   <div class="def"><b>Token</b><p>One named design value — a colour, size, font, duration, breakpoint — authored once in the W3C Design Tokens format (DTCG JSON, under <span class="k">packages/tokens/src/tokens/</span>) and compiled to every platform. Components never contain raw values; they reference tokens.</p></div>
+   <p>One build (<span class="k">pnpm build:tokens</span>, Style Dictionary) compiles the JSON sources into five outputs: CSS custom properties (916 variables), a TypeScript map, a Tailwind token map, a Swift file and a Kotlin file. Tokens are layered so that a change at the right level propagates:</p>
+ <pre><code>--ref-color-neutral-50:       oklch(0.9913 0.0013 286.38)   <span class="cmt">/* reference: a raw value            */</span>
+ --theme-surface-canvas:       var(--ref-color-neutral-50)   <span class="cmt">/* theme: a role in this theme       */</span>
+ --sys-surface-canvas:         var(--theme-surface-canvas)   <span class="cmt">/* system: what components bind to   */</span>
+ --cmp-text-placeholder-color: var(--sys-text-muted)         <span class="cmt">/* component: one slot on one widget */</span>
+ --sys-breakpoint-md:          768                           <span class="cmt">/* breakpoints are tokens too        */</span></code></pre>
+   <p><b>Themes</b> are light, dark and high-contrast (the last maps to the operating system's forced colours). <b>Brands</b> are sets of values for the brand colour roles — surfaces, text, interactive, borders, status and focus — one JSON file per brand per theme (44 leaves each, under <span class="k">brands/&lt;X&gt;/{base,dark,hc}.json</span>). The build emits a block per brand × theme that re-assigns 41 shared theme slots to that brand's values:</p>
+ <pre><code>[data-brand='B'][data-theme='dark'] { --theme-surface-canvas: …; --theme-text-primary: …; … }</code></pre>
+   <p>So switching brand or theme is two attributes on the document; no component changes. Two brands ship today (A and B); three brand-relative presets (corporate-blue, startup-warm, dark-minimal) can be applied to any brand. <span class="k">brand.intake</span> validates inline brand-relative DTCG documents and returns an envelope hash plus a consumable delta for accepted A/B themes. <span class="k">brand.apply</span> previews that delta or writes canonical brand source and runs both token-build stages, retaining source hashes, byte counts and captured build results in a dated review folder. A failed build reports the output tail and leaves source writes in place. <span class="k">tokens.build</span> returns requested-scope JSON and CSS alongside full CSS and legacy A/light TypeScript and Tailwind artifacts.</p>
+   <p><b>Guards around the build.</b> A collision guard refuses to build if two source files declare the same token with different values; 228 brand-contrast rules (57 text/icon pairs per brand per theme) run in CI, and any theme slot a brand newly overrides must be graded; a real-browser check asserts the computed values in all six brand × theme cells; a CI check blocks high-risk token changes (by namespace and segment) unless the PR carries the <span class="k">token-change:breaking</span> label.</p>
+
+   <!-- 8 -->
+   <h2 id="generate"><span class="n">08</span>Generating screens and code</h2>
+   <p>The generate path is four tools that chain, plus one that runs them together. They pass a <b>UiSchema</b> between them:</p>
+   <div class="def"><b>UiSchema</b><p>A JSON tree of components. Every node is <span class="k">{ id, component, layout, style, props, bindings, children, meta }</span>; the root carries <span class="k">version</span>, <span class="k">theme</span>, <span class="k">screens[]</span>, and <span class="k">tokenOverrides</span> (the traits' token map from section 02).</p></div>
+ <pre><code>{ "version": "1.0", "theme": "light",
+   "screens": [ { "id": "product-detail", "component": "Page",
+       "layout": { "type": "stack", "gapToken": "space.4" },
+       "children": [
+         { "id": "header", "component": "DetailHeader", "props": { "field": "label" }, "bindings": { "title": "label" } },
+         { "id": "status", "component": "StatusBadge",  "props": { "field": "status" }, "meta": { "confidence": 0.92 } }
+       ] } ] }</code></pre>
+   <div class="tw"><table>
+     <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>In</th><th>Out</th></tr></thead>
+     <tbody>
+       <tr><td>design.compose</td><td>A short intent ("order history list with filters") and/or an object + context (detail, list, form, timeline, card, inline), optional layout (dashboard, form, detail, list, card, timeline, landing, auto) and component overrides.</td><td>A UiSchema plus a reference id (30-minute lifetime), per-slot confidence, and a review hint where a slot was uncertain. Object-aware: it fills slots from the object's fields and view extensions and reports which traits it used.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>repl · validate</td><td>A UiSchema, or a patch to one (JSON Patch or per-node replace).</td><td>Structural validation against the schema grammar and the component catalog; optionally the 18 token-contrast rules.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>repl · render</td><td>A UiSchema (its <span class="k">theme</span> sets the theme); optional brand (A|B).</td><td>A self-contained HTML document (or per-component fragments) with tokens inlined and <span class="k">data-brand</span>/<span class="k">data-theme</span> set.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>code.generate</td><td>A validated UiSchema; framework react | vue | html; styling inline | tokens | tailwind; TypeScript on/off.</td><td>One source file as a string (.tsx / .vue / .html) plus its import list. React imports the real <span class="k">@oods/components-react</span> package, Vue imports <span class="k">@oods/components-vue</span>, and both import <span class="k">@oods/component-styles/css</span>. A known component/target pair that is not emission-eligible fails before emission with typed error <span class="k">OODS-N015</span> and no source payload; HTML remains standalone.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>pipeline</td><td>The same intent/object input.</td><td>compose → validate → render → code in one call; optional accessibility check and save.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>schema · save/load/list/delete</td><td>A schema reference.</td><td>Persists work past the 30-minute reference lifetime and across sessions.</td></tr>
+     </tbody>
+   </table></div>
+   <p>Intent matching is rule-based (keywords and synonyms choose the layout; a deterministic selector picks components), so the same input always produces the same tree — there is no language model inside Forge. A real run: intent "product detail page" auto-detects the Product object, picks the detail layout, and returns a 31-node tree with 10 filled slots — a header with the detail header, status timeline, search input and price summary; eight tabs expanded from Product's 38 fields; a metadata sidebar with the classification panel and filter panel.</p>
+   <p>Alongside: <span class="k">fidelity.preview</span> renders an object manifest at four fidelities (boxes-and-arrows, wireframe, review, branded mockup, using built A/B light tokens); deprecated brand-a/brand-b aliases remain for one release and unknown brands fail explicitly. <span class="k">map</span> records and resolves mappings for external consumers; no composer or generator consumes them. Draft preferred terms and disambiguation decisions are surfaced, not consumed by resolution; <span class="k">structuredData.fetch</span> serves the components and tokens datasets and <span class="k">registry.snapshot</span> serves traits, objects and mappings, all versioned with ETags.</p>
+
+   <!-- 9 -->
+   <h2 id="charts"><span class="n">09</span>Charts and dashboards</h2>
+   <p><span class="k">viz.render</span> takes data and returns a chart specification. Thirteen chart types: <b>bar, line, area, scatter, heatmap</b> (from tabular rows; compiled to Vega-Lite, ECharts on request) and <b>treemap, sunburst, sankey, chord, force graph, choropleth, bubble map, flow map</b> (from node/link/geometry inputs; ECharts). Three input modes: explicit (chart type + encodings), suggest (rows only — the recommender picks), or a structured intent (goal, measures, dimensions). Every result carries a content hash of the canonical spec; on request it also returns an accessible data table and a generated narrative derived from the same data. The 16 chart traits (marks, encodings, scales, layouts, interactions) are the vocabulary the spec is normalised into.</p>
+   <p><span class="k">dashboard.render</span> composes a metric overview — KPI tiles, a trend panel, a breakdown panel, optional geo — from shared datasets and panel definitions, with deterministic auto-layout and cross-filter linking, and can export one self-contained HTML file with server-rendered SVG for all 13 registered chart types: Vega-Lite for the five Cartesian families and ECharts for the eight primary families. Failed panels retain explicit error placeholders; a successful chart panel must have a renderable specification.</p>
+
+   <!-- 10 -->
+   <h2 id="certify"><span class="n">10</span>Certification and checks</h2>
+   <div class="def"><b>Certify</b><p><span class="k">artifact.certify</span> takes a chart specification back (and, for the advanced types, its data) and returns a per-check scorecard — pass, fail or unchecked with the reason — rather than an overall "looks fine". Its four core certification pillars are accessibility equivalence, determinism, contrast and accuracy.</p></div>
+   <div class="tw"><table>
+     <thead><tr><th>Core certification pillar</th><th>What is actually tested</th></tr></thead>
+     <tbody>
+       <tr><td>Accessibility equivalence</td><td>16 structural rules over the chart and its text alternative: colour encodings carry a second cue and size differences are perceptible; an accessible table exists, captioned to the chart title, with declared column order; axis titles, an accessible name and a description over 25 characters are present; bar/stacked charts narrate extrema, trend charts narrate the trend, area charts describe their baseline/range; datasets with 3+ rows surface at least two findings and dense ones aggregate; every encoded field is present in every row; the narrative generator reports ready; interactions describe their announcements. The chart builder synthesises the table, narrative, axis titles and name itself, so generated charts pass by construction; error-level failures block emission.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>Determinism</td><td>The spec is compiled twice, canonicalised and byte-compared; the resulting SHA-256 is the same hash <span class="k">viz.render</span> produced.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>Contrast</td><td>The colour values actually baked into the spec are graded: every mark at least 3:1 against the requested light or dark theme canvas (WCAG 1.4.11); categorical palettes checked for distinguishability under normal vision and three colour-vision-deficiency simulations; gradient scales reported as exempt, not passed. The requested A/B brand and light/dark theme are retained; high-contrast server pixels are unsupported.</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>10 structural-distortion rules: bar baseline not at zero (or on log/sqrt); dual y-axes in one frame; area on a non-linear scale; a row-merging aggregation not disclosed in title, description or axis; negative treemap/sunburst values; a parent not equal to the sum of its children; negative sankey/chord links; sankey node height inconsistent with its flows; duplicate sankey links; conflicting values joined to one map region. "Pass" means none of these fired, and the count of rules that actually ran is reported.</td></tr>
+     </tbody>
+   </table></div>
+   <p><b>Coverage, stated plainly.</b> The five core types get a rolled-up verdict. The eight advanced types get each check separately — contrast always, determinism and accuracy when data is supplied, accessibility-equivalence reported as findings but not yet enforced — and are labelled "uncertified" on purpose. Certification covers chart specs, not pages or components. It accepts brand A or B and theme light or dark (defaults A/light), matching rendering and contrast grading. High-contrast token exports exist, but server-side high-contrast chart pixels are unsupported.</p>
+   <h3>Other checks</h3>
+   <ul>
+     <li><b>Token contrast</b> — <span class="k">a11y.scan</span> (and <span class="k">repl validate</span> on request) grades 18 token pairs: primary text on canvas / raised / subtle surfaces, secondary, muted, accent and inverse text, the four status text and four status icon pairs, and three dark-theme primary-text variants — 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for icons.</li>
+     <li><b>Component library in CI</b> — axe-core (WCAG 2.0 A + 2.1 AA, serious/critical) over a fixed set of Storybook stories in light and dark; Playwright behavioural checks at phone (375), tablet (768) and desktop widths (layout collapse, drawer focus trap, Escape to close, no violations with the drawer open); the brand-contrast and browser checks from section 07; optional Chromatic visual regression.</li>
+     <li><b>Native tokens</b> — the Swift and Kotlin outputs are compiled with the real toolchains before a release (a shape test runs in CI).</li>
+   </ul>
+
+   <!-- 11 -->
+   <h2 id="operate"><span class="n">11</span>Operating it: starting, connecting, tools</h2>
+   <p><b>Start and connect.</b> Build the server once: <span class="k">pnpm --filter @oods/mcp-server run build</span>. Connect an AI assistant with the stdio adapter — copy <span class="k">configs/agents/claude-desktop.stdio-mcp.json</span> into Claude Desktop's MCP config, or <span class="k">cursor.stdio-mcp.json</span> into <span class="k">.cursor/mcp.json</span>. For a browser panel or HTTP-style agents, run the bridge (<span class="k">pnpm bridge:dev</span>; it listens on <span class="k">127.0.0.1:4466</span>). From the terminal, call any tool directly: <span class="k">pnpm exec tsx tools/oods-agent-cli/src/index.ts plan design.compose '{"intent":"product detail page"}'</span>. The authoritative roster is <span class="k">packages/mcp-server/src/tools/registry.json</span>: 19 tools register by default and 6 more switch on with <span class="k">MCP_TOOLSET=all</span>.</p>
+   <div class="tw"><table>
+     <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Tools</th></tr></thead>
+     <tbody>
+       <tr><td>Registry</td><td>catalog.list · object (list, show) · structuredData.fetch · registry.snapshot · health</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>Generate &amp; review</td><td>design.compose · design.preview · repl (validate, render) · code.generate · pipeline · schema (save, load, list, delete) · fidelity.preview · map (create, list, resolve, update, delete, apply)</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>Charts</td><td>viz.render · dashboard.render · artifact.certify</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>Tokens &amp; brand</td><td>tokens.build · brand.apply · brand.intake</td></tr>
+       <tr><td>On demand</td><td>a11y.scan · diag.snapshot · billing.reviewKit · billing.switchFixtures · release.verify · release.tag</td></tr>
+     </tbody>
+   </table></div>
+   <p><b>Safety model.</b> Tools that write into the repo or artifacts (tokens.build, brand.apply, a11y.scan, billing.*, map.*, diag.snapshot, release.tag) preview by default and write only with <span class="k">apply: true</span>; schema save persists on call and pipeline writes via its save option. Two roles (designer, maintainer — release tools are maintainer-only); per-tool rate limits, concurrency and timeouts from a policy file; outputs land under <span class="k">artifacts/current-state/&lt;date&gt;/</span> with redacted transcripts and a hashed bundle index. Optional OpenTelemetry tracing per tool call.</p>
+   <p><b>Everyday commands:</b> <span class="k">pnpm build:tokens</span> · <span class="k">pnpm storybook</span> · <span class="k">pnpm refresh:data</span> (regenerate the structured-data exports the registry tools serve) · <span class="k">pnpm object:list | object:compose &lt;Name&gt; | object:validate --compose | object:create &lt;Name&gt; --trait … | object:generate</span> · <span class="k">pnpm validate --path traits</span> · <span class="k">pnpm local:pr-check</span> (lint, typecheck, unit tests, accessibility contract, visual-regression dry run).</p>
+
+   <!-- 12 -->
+   <h2 id="extend"><span class="n">12</span>Extending it — and what it does not do</h2>
+   <ul>
+     <li><b>New trait:</b> <span class="k">traits/&lt;family&gt;/&lt;Name&gt;.trait.yaml</span> (the suffix is required) plus <span class="k">schemas/traits/&lt;name&gt;.parameters.schema.json</span>, and the components its view extensions name; <span class="k">pnpm validate --path traits</span> checks the library. Runbook: <span class="k">docs/authoring-traits.md</span>.</li>
+     <li><b>New object:</b> <span class="k">objects/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;Name&gt;.object.yaml</span> (or a domain pack) naming traits and parameters; <span class="k">pnpm object:create</span> scaffolds it; the server indexes it on its next start. Runbook: <span class="k">docs/authoring-objects.md</span>.</li>
+     <li><b>New domain:</b> a folder under <span class="k">domains/</span> with its own traits and objects (the SaaS-billing pack is the model).</li>
+     <li><b>New brand:</b> <span class="k">packages/tokens/src/tokens/brands/&lt;X&gt;/{base,dark,hc}.json</span> plus <span class="k">aliases/brand-&lt;X&gt;.json</span> and entries in the build config (<span class="k">style-dictionary.config.cjs</span>: brand scopes and shared base list); the tool schemas' brand enum and the CI matrix are widened; <span class="k">pnpm build:tokens</span> then emits the brand's blocks, and the contrast rules and browser check cover it.</li>
+     <li><b>New components:</b> React source + story, a renderer entry in <span class="k">packages/mcp-server/src/render/component-map.ts</span>, and a trait view extension naming it (primitives are listed in the refresh script); <span class="k">pnpm refresh:data</span> republishes the catalog the tools read.</li>
+     <li><b>An outside component library:</b> describe it with <span class="k">map</span> (component → trait, prop translations) so its vocabulary resolves to trait terms via <span class="k">map resolve</span> and <span class="k">registry.snapshot</span>.</li>
+   </ul>
+   <h3>What Forge does not do</h3>
+   <ul>
+     <li>No data storage or backend — objects are definitions, not records; there is no runtime data binding.</li>
+     <li>No language model inside — intent matching is rules and synonyms; the AI assistant sits outside and calls tools.</li>
+     <li>No hosted service — the server reads a full checkout and the bridge binds to localhost.</li>
+     <li>Certification covers chart specs only, scoped to the requested A/B brand and light/dark theme; the eight advanced chart types are "uncertified" by design; pages and components get contrast rules and CI audits, not certificates.</li>
+     <li>Generated HTML documents use the tokens and components but not the six-region responsive shell; code generation targets React, Vue and HTML only.</li>
+     <li>Fonts are not yet per-brand (one global stack); the chart palette is one fixed set; the Swift/Kotlin files carry one brand × theme and omit font stacks and easing curves.</li>
+   </ul>
+
+   <footer class="foot">Figures are from the Forge repository in August 2026 (41 traits · 11 objects · ~100 catalogued components · 8 contexts · 13 chart types). The 27-tool roster comes from <span class="k">packages/mcp-server/src/tools/registry.json</span>; contracts: <span class="k">packages/mcp-server/src/schemas/</span> and <span class="k">docs/api/</span>.</footer>
+ </div>
+

